course: everyday
lesson: 202
title: Rumours and gossip
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A rumour is a story passed from person to person without anyone checking whether it is true. Like the game «Telephone» (Chinese whispers), a message changes each time it is passed on, so second-hand stories are often wrong.
explain: Even a true piece of gossip can hurt badly: a crush, a private message, a whisper about someone in the room. Online, gossip spreads even faster and stays around.
explain: The wise habits: ask «How do you know that?», do not pass it on, check important news with a teacher or parent, and judge people by how they treat you yourself.
explain: Example: a rumour spreads in a school in Phoenix that a scary character is coming to school. Such viral scares are usually hoaxes. A frightened child tells an adult, who checks and reassures her.
explain: If you passed on something untrue, go back to the people you told, say it was not true, and say sorry to the person it was about.
fact: Messages change each time they are passed on, so second-hand stories are often wrong.
fact: Many rumours are untrue or twisted, and repeating one makes you part of the harm.
fact: Asking «How do you know that?» helps you test a rumour.
fact: Rumours about crushes can embarrass people badly and spread fast.
fact: Check news like «school is closed tomorrow» with a teacher, the school website or your parents.
fact: Online gossip spreads fast, stays around and can really hurt someone.
fact: Private messages were meant for one person, and spreading them betrays trust.
fact: Judge people by how they treat you, not by gossip.
fact: People trust someone who keeps secrets more than someone who spreads gossip.
fact: Correcting a rumour you passed on helps undo the harm.
fact: Viral scary-character and «challenge» rumours are usually hoaxes, and an adult can check if there is real danger.
fact: Whispering and giggling about someone who is present makes them feel left out and talked about.
exercise: You hear that a classmate «likes» someone. What should you do? | Not pass it on | Tell the person she likes | Tell your group chat | Ask her about it in front of everyone
exercise: A friend tells you something bad about another child. What is a wise question to ask? | «How do you know that?» | «Who else can we tell?» | «Is there more to the story?» | «Should we put it online?»
exercise: In the game «Telephone», the message changes completely. What does it teach about rumours? | Second-hand stories are often wrong | Rumours always get more accurate as more people repeat them | Only the first person can lie | Messages never really change
exercise: There is a rumour that school is closed tomorrow. What should you do? | Check with a teacher, the school website or your parents | Stay at home just in case | Tell everyone so they stay at home too | Believe it, since lots of different people in your class said it
exercise: A group chat starts making fun of a classmate. What is right? | Do not join in; leave the chat or tell an adult | Add a funny picture to join in | Read it all but say nothing to anyone | Share it with a friend in another class so they can see it too
exercise: A friend shows you a screenshot of someone's private messages. What should you do? | Not share it any further | Send it to everyone in your class | Reply to the person to tease them | Keep it to use later
exercise: A new kid arrives and people whisper about him. What is fair? | Get to know him yourself and make up your own mind | Believe the whispers, since the others have known him much longer | Stay away until the whispers stop | Add your own story to the whispers
exercise: Someone says a teacher is «mean» and has a secret. How should you judge the teacher? | By how she treats you, not by gossip | By what the oldest children say | By the rumours, since they are usually right | By asking her about the secret
exercise: You know a juicy secret and could tell it to seem interesting. What is wise? | Keep it to yourself | Tell just one or two people | Tell it but say it is a secret | Tell it only to people who will laugh
exercise: You find out a rumour you passed on was untrue. What should you do? | Tell the people you told that it was false, and say sorry to the person | Hope everyone forgets about it soon | Say someone else started it | Start a new and even bigger rumour so that everyone forgets about the first
exercise: A rumour says a scary character will come to school. What is true? | Such rumours are usually hoaxes; tell an adult if you feel frightened | You should stay home until it is over | It must be true if it is on the internet and lots of children have seen it | Telling an adult will make it worse
exercise: Friends whisper and giggle while looking at a girl nearby. What should you do? | Not join in whispering about someone who is there | Join in so you are not left out | Tell the girl exactly what they are saying about her behind her back | Whisper something even funnier
test: A boy in Edinburgh hears from a cousin's friend that a classmate was arrested. Why should he doubt it? | Stories change as they pass along, and he has no evidence | Cousins' friends always tell the truth | Arrests are always reported at school | He should believe it if it sounds exciting and comes from someone he knows
test: Why can repeating even a TRUE piece of gossip be wrong? | It can still embarrass and hurt the person | True things can never hurt anyone | Because true gossip is always boring | Because only adults may share news
test: A girl hears that a nasty video about a classmate is going round. What is the most helpful thing? | Not watch or share it, and tell a trusted adult | Watch it so she knows what is going on and can tell the others | Share it to warn other children | Comment that it is not very funny
test: A message says «Text this to ten friends or a monster will visit you tonight.» What is it? | A hoax meant to scare and spread | A real warning from the police | Something to ignore unless you have pets | A message you must send to stay safe
test: Ella told three friends a rumour about Sam, then learned it was false. What should she do? | Tell the three friends it was false and apologise to Sam | Tell nobody, since it is now in the past | Tell nobody, since it is now in the past and everyone will forget it | Start a different rumour about someone else
test: Why do people trust someone who keeps secrets? | They know their secrets will not be spread | Because that person never talks to anyone | Because keeping secrets makes you popular | Because they hope to hear gossip later
test: Everyone says a new teacher in Vancouver is strict and unfair, but she is kind to you. What should you believe? | Your own experience of how she treats you | What the most children say about her | That she is only pretending to be kind | That you must have done something special
test: A friend forwards you a private message another girl sent to her. What is the problem with passing it on? | It was meant for one person, so spreading it betrays trust | Private messages are too long to read | It is only a problem if the message is rude | There is no problem if it is only a screenshot and not the real message
test: someone said school's shut tmrw, its true right? | Check with a teacher, the school website or your parents | Yes, everyone's saying it | Yes, tell everyone you know so that nobody turns up by mistake | Yes, stay home to be safe
test: heard Mia fancies Tom, gonna tell everyone | Don't pass it on, it can embarrass her badly | Tell everyone, it's just a bit of fun and he'll find out anyway | Tell only half the class | Post it online first
test: told ppl a rumour, now i know its not true | Correct it with the people you told | Say nothing and hope it's forgotten | Make up a new rumour | Blame the person who told you
test: theres a scary character challenge goin round, kids r freaked | It's usually a hoax; an adult can check if there's real danger | It's real, hide | Do the challenge to see | Share it so others know
explain: A girl in Texas passed on a rumour that a classmate had stolen money; it was not true, but other children stopped sitting with the classmate for weeks, and she cried every morning before school.
explain: A boy in Dublin shared a made-up story about a teacher in a group chat; it spread to parents, the school had to deal with it, and the boy was suspended (sent home from school for some days).
fact: A rumour can spread to hundreds of people in a day online, and the truth hardly ever catches up with it.
fact: Being the target of rumours can make a child lonely, anxious and afraid to go to school.
fact: Gossip often uses grown-up words: «fancies» means likes someone in a romantic way, «seeing someone» means going out with them as a couple, and parents «splitting up» means they are separating; these are private matters, not things to spread.
test: heard Sam nicked money from the bag, should i tell the others | No, don't pass it on, it might not be true and can really hurt him | Yes, people need warning | Yes, but only to your best mates | Only tell them if you add that you're not sure and it might be made up
test: A false rumour about Lily spread round the class. What is a likely result? | Others avoid her and she feels lonely and scared | Nobody really believes rumours, so nothing happens to her at all | She becomes more popular | It is forgotten by lunch time
test: there's a nasty story about me going round the group chat, what do i do | Screenshot it and tell a parent or teacher | Post a nastier one about them so they know how it feels | Leave school for a week | Keep it secret so it dies out
test: omg everyone's saying school's shut tomorrow cos of a burst pipe, my mate's brother's friend heard it. what do u do? | Check with the school website, a teacher or a parent | Stay home, so many people can't be wrong | Post it on the class chat to be helpful | Ask the friend's brother to swear it's true
test: apparently that girl in 5B got expelled for stealing AND her family's moving to Spain, I swear. which bit is likely just rumour? | All of it; second-hand stories get twisted each time | Only the Spain part; expulsions are always real | Only the stealing; families often move to Spain | None of it, since lots of people said it
test: did u hear about the scary doll thing on the internet that comes into ur room at night?? kids are freaking out. real lesson? | Such viral scares are usually hoaxes; ask an adult | Lock your door every night, just in case | Send the warning to every friend you have | Stop using any computer or tablet at all
test: apparently Miss Parker's seeing the PE teacher, someone saw them at the cinema!! what does "seeing" mean here and what's right? | They might be a couple; it's their private business, so don't spread it | She looks at him a lot, so she needs glasses | They are planning a school trip to the cinema | It means they're in trouble, so tell the head teacher
test: "Jack fancies Emily, everyone knows, let's sing it at break" — what does fancies mean and should you join in? | Jack likes Emily in a romantic way; no, teasing about it hurts both of them | Jack wants to be Emily's maths partner; yes, it's harmless | Jack is jealous of Emily's clothes; yes, songs are fun | Jack thinks Emily is a fan of his; yes, everyone's doing it
test: some kid said "her mum and dad are splitting up, my nan said, that's why she's weird lately" — what does it mean and what do you do? | Her parents may be separating; don't spread it, be kind to her | Her parents are moving to two houses for a holiday | She is weird, so stay away from her | Ask her in front of everyone if it's true
exam: Identify the principal reason for which second-hand stories are frequently inaccurate. | A message is altered each time it is passed on | Persons who repeat stories invariably intend to lie | Stories become more accurate with each telling | Rumours are generally fabricated by adults
exam: Which of the following constitutes the most prudent response upon hearing that the school is to be closed on the following day? | To verify the report with a teacher, the school website or a parent | To accept the report if it is repeated by several pupils | To forward the report to all acquaintances forthwith | To remain at home without further inquiry
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the private messages of another person. | Disseminating them betrays the trust of the sender | They may be shared freely once received | They may be shared if the sender is no longer a friend | Sharing them is harmless if done online
exam: Ascertain which question most effectively tests the veracity of a rumour. | «How do you know that?» | «Who else has heard this?» | «Is it not exciting?» | «May I tell my friends?»
